  "chapter_number": 1,
  "chapter_title": "Patterns in Mathematics",
  "sections": [
    { "ref": "section 1.1", "title": "What is Mathematics?", "section_goal": ["recall"] },
    { "ref": "section 1.2", "title": "Patterns in Numbers", "section_goal": ["recall"] },
    { "ref": "section 1.3", "title": "Visualising Number Sequences", "section_goal": ["reason"] },
    { "ref": "section 1.4", "title": "Relations among Number Sequences", "section_goal": ["reason"] },
    { "ref": "section 1.5", "title": "Patterns in Shapes", "section_goal": ["recall"] },
    { "ref": "section 1.6", "title": "Relation to Number Sequences", "section_goal": ["reason"] }
  ],
  "prose_summary": "Section 1.1 introduces mathematics as, in large part, the search for patterns and the explanations for why those patterns exist. The section positions this search as both an art and a science — pattern-finding is framed as creative and aesthetic, not merely computational. The text connects mathematical reasoning to its real-world consequences: understanding patterns in planetary motion led to the theory of gravitation and space exploration; patterns in genomes have enabled disease diagnosis and treatment. Students are invited to reflect on further everyday applications of mathematics and to articulate how the discipline has propelled human progress.\n\nSection 1.2 identifies number sequences as the most basic and foundational type of mathematical pattern. The branch of mathematics that studies patterns in whole numbers (0, 1, 2, 3, 4, …) is named as number theory. Ten named sequences are presented in Table 1: all 1's, counting numbers, odd numbers, even numbers, triangular numbers, squares, cubes, Virahānka numbers, powers of 2, and powers of 3. Students are asked first to recognise the formation rule behind each sequence and then to extend each sequence by three further terms, articulating the rule in their own words.\n\nSection 1.3 explains that number sequences can often be visualised using pictures or dot diagrams, and that such visualisation is a powerful tool for understanding patterns and the relationships between them. Table 2 shows dot-based pictorial representations of the first seven sequences from Table 1 — all 1's, counting numbers, odd numbers, even numbers, triangular numbers, squares, and cubes — and the growth of each sequence is mirrored in the expanding geometry of its dot arrangement. The five Figure it Out questions ask students to copy and extend each picture, explain the geometric reasoning behind the names 'triangular numbers,' 'square numbers,' and 'cubes,' notice that 36 is simultaneously both triangular and square, identify the hexagonal number sequence (1, 7, 19, 37, …) from a given dot picture, and find pictorial ways to represent powers of 2 and powers of 3.\n\nSection 1.4 demonstrates that number sequences can be related to each other in surprising and illuminating ways. A worked example shows that summing successive odd numbers produces square numbers: 1 = 1, 1+3 = 4, 1+3+5 = 9, and so on. The explanation is visual: a square dot grid of side n can always be partitioned into successive L-shaped gnomons containing 1, 3, 5, 7, … dots, making the pattern universal and provable rather than merely observed. A second pattern — 'adding up and down,' where the sums 1, 1+2+1, 1+2+3+2+1, … also produce square numbers — is introduced as another instance of this cross-sequence relationship, and students are prompted to find a similar pictorial explanation for it. The nine Figure it Out questions in this section build extensively on these two patterns: students compute the sums of the first 10 and 100 odd numbers using the visual insight, determine the value of the symmetric sum 1 + 2 + 3 + … + 100 + … + 2 + 1, investigate what sequences arise from successively adding all 1's and counting numbers, explore the connection between pairs of consecutive triangular numbers and squares, discover what the running sums of powers of 2 yield and why adding 1 to each gives the next power, connect triangular numbers to hexagonal numbers via multiplication by 6, and find that partial sums of hexagonal numbers produce cubes.\n\nSection 1.5 introduces shape sequences as a second basic and fundamental category of mathematical pattern. The branch of mathematics that studies patterns in shapes — in one, two, or three (or more) dimensions — is named as geometry. Table 3 presents five shape sequences: regular polygons (triangle through decagon), complete graphs (K2 through K6, where every node connects to every other), stacked squares (growing n×n grids of unit squares), stacked triangles (growing triangular figures composed of smaller triangles), and the Koch snowflake (an iterative sequence in which each line segment is replaced by a four-segment speed-bump shape). Students are asked to identify the rule behind each shape sequence and to draw the next shape — a task that becomes progressively more intricate for the Koch snowflake.\n\nSection 1.6 establishes that shape sequences encode number sequences in equally surprising ways. A worked example identifies that the regular polygon sequence encodes the counting numbers starting at 3 (the triangle has 3 sides, the quadrilateral 4, the pentagon 5, and so on), and that the number of corners in each regular polygon matches its number of sides because in any closed polygon the two counts are always equal. The five Figure it Out questions ask students to verify and reason about the number sequences hidden in the other shape sequences of Table 3: complete graphs encode triangular numbers (1, 3, 6, 10, 15) because each new node connects to all previous ones; stacked squares encode square numbers (1, 4, 9, 16, 25) because each shape is an n×n grid; stacked triangles also encode square numbers via the same 'adding up and down' principle established in section 1.4; and the Koch snowflake encodes 3 times the powers of 4 (3, 12, 48, 192, …) because each line segment is replaced by four segments at every iteration.",
