  "prose_summary": "Section 7.1 identifies fractional units through the familiar context of sharing rotis equally, establishing that when one unit is divided into equal parts each part is a fractional unit (unit fraction). The section opens with a dialogue exploring why 1/2 > 1/4 — more children sharing the same roti means each gets less — and extends this reasoning to compare any two unit fractions. Students recognise that the larger the denominator, the smaller the fractional unit, and practice ordering everyday fraction words from smallest to largest.\n\nSection 7.2 identifies how fractions arise as parts of a whole, using a chikki (peanut brittle) as the physical referent. Students see that the same whole can be divided into equal parts of different shapes yet identical size, reinforcing that the fractional unit depends on the number of equal parts rather than their shape. The section grounds the concept that a fraction like 3/4 means three parts each of size 1/4 of the whole.\n\nSection 7.3 defines how fractional quantities are measured by collecting fractional units, then applies this by paper-strip folding to produce fractions with denominators 2, 4, and 8. Students repeatedly fold a unit strip, name the resulting fractional units, and build repeated-addition statements (e.g., 3 times 1/4 = 3/4). The section then introduces the numerator–denominator vocabulary explicitly, explaining that reading 3/4 as '3 times 1/4' communicates size more transparently than merely saying 'three quarters'.\n\nSection 7.4 applies fraction knowledge to the number line, marking lengths less than and greater than one using unit intervals divided into equal parts. Students plot fractions with denominators 3, 5, and 8, and discover that the number of fractions between 0 and 1 is infinite. The section also introduces fractions greater than 1 on the number line, setting the stage for mixed fractions, and exercises require writing fractions that match coloured line-segment lengths.\n\nSection 7.5 recalls fractions greater than one, then applies conversions between improper fractions and mixed numbers in both directions. Students first identify how many whole units are contained in a given improper fraction, decompose the remainder, and write the result as a mixed number (e.g., 8/3 = 2 and 2/3). The section then applies the reverse: converting a mixed number such as 3¼ back to an improper fraction by reconstructing it as a sum of unit fractions. Both procedures are practised through Figure it Out exercises covering a wide range of examples.\n\nSection 7.6 reasons about equivalent fractions through two complementary lenses — equal fractional lengths on a paper fraction wall, and equal shares of rotis among children. The fraction-wall approach establishes that 1/2 = 2/4 = 4/8 visually; the equal-shares approach explains why doubling both numerator and denominator preserves value. Students then apply these ideas to find missing numerators or denominators, express fractions in lowest terms by dividing by the highest common factor (using worked examples like 16/20 → 4/5 and 36/60 → 3/5), and compare fractions such as 1/2 vs 5/8 by converting to a common denominator. A short comparison puzzle about which group of children receives more chikki formalises the rule: same numerator, larger denominator means smaller share; same denominator, larger numerator means larger share.\n\nSection 7.7 applies fraction comparison systematically using the common-denominator method. Two fractions (4/5 and 7/9, then 7/9 and 17/21) are compared by rewriting them with the product of their denominators as the common denominator and then comparing numerators. A summary box formalises the two-step method: (1) find equivalent fractions with the same denominator; (2) compare numerators. Exercises include ordering three fractions in ascending and descending order.\n\nSection 7.8 reasons about and then applies Brahmagupta's method for adding and subtracting fractions. The section opens visually with a chikki-sharing story and rectangular-strip models to show why 1/2 + 1/4 = 3/4 (converting 1/2 to 2/4 and then summing). Addition with unlike denominators is handled by finding a common multiple of the denominators, rewriting each fraction, and summing numerators. Brahmagupta's three-step procedure is stated explicitly and credited to its 628 CE source. Subtraction follows the identical logic — same-denominator cases first, then unlike denominators via equivalent fractions. Exercises range from pure computation to word problems involving paint volumes, lace lengths, and travel distances.\n\nSection 7.9 recalls the ancient Indian origins of fraction notation, noting that the bhinna/bhaga terminology predates the Arabic–European transmission and that Brahmagupta's formal codification in 628 CE is still the method used today. The section offers an enrichment puzzle: expressing 1 as a sum of distinct fractional units. Students reason that two distinct unit fractions cannot sum to 1, find the unique three-unit-fraction solution (1/2 + 1/3 + 1/6 = 1), and are challenged to find the six four-unit-fraction solutions.",
