  "prose_summary": "Section 8.1 applies ruler and compass techniques to create freehand-inspired artwork figures — a person, a wavy wave, and a pair of eyes — introducing the compass as a precision drawing tool. The section opens by inviting students to replicate visual figures freehand, then challenges them to reproduce the same figures using only a ruler and compass. In doing so it establishes the core idea of a circle: all points on a circle are the same distance from its centre, and that distance is the radius. Students set the compass opening to 4 cm and trace every point 4 cm from a fixed centre P, discovering that the resulting curve is a circle. They then apply this understanding to construct composite artwork figures: the wavy wave is built from sequential half-circles drawn on a baseline; the 'A Person' figure combines circles and arcs; the eyes are constructed using arcs with carefully estimated compass placements. Each construction requires students to determine suitable centre positions and radii by trial and estimation rather than by formula, building spatial reasoning and instrument fluency. A Figure it Out set asks about the required radius and AX length for the wavy wave, and a Try This prompt asks students to recreate smaller, identical waves.\n\nSection 8.2 recalls the defining properties of squares and rectangles from prior knowledge, then identifies these properties through measurement and visual reasoning on dot-grid figures. The section names the parts of a rectangle ABCD — corners, sides, opposite sides, and angles — and states the two rectangle properties: opposite sides are equal (R1) and all angles are 90° (R2). The two square properties follow analogously: all sides equal (S1) and all angles 90° (S2). The section introduces valid naming conventions for rectangles and squares, explaining that a valid name requires corners to appear in order of travel around the figure, and presents a brief puzzle — which of four names is not valid for a given square. A key conceptual moment concerns rotated squares and rectangles: rotating does not change side lengths or angles, so a rotated square is still a square and a rotated rectangle is still a rectangle. Figure it Out exercises ask students to draw configurations on dot paper, identify squares by measurement among given figures, and draw rotated squares and rectangles on dot grids.\n\nSection 8.3 applies the rectangle and square properties to construct these figures with ruler and compass. The section walks through a six-step construction of a square PQRS of side 6 cm: draw PQ, raise a perpendicular at P (by two methods — ruler and compass), mark S on the perpendicular at distance 6 cm, raise a perpendicular at Q, and use the compass to mark R at the correct distance. Students observe that RS must equal 6 cm and that angles at R and S are each 90°, confirming both square properties. Construct exercises ask students to draw a 4 cm × 6 cm rectangle, a 2 cm × 10 cm rectangle, and to reason whether a quadrilateral with all 90° angles but unequal opposite sides is possible.\n\nSection 8.4 reasons about how the distance between a movable point X on side AD and a movable point Y on side BC of rectangle ABCD changes with position, then applies this insight to construct special rectangles. The section first poses the investigation as a Math Talk: at which positions are X and Y closest and farthest? Students fill a table tracking XY for various positions of X and Y and discover that when X and Y are equidistant from A and B respectively, XY equals AB and ABYX becomes a rectangle. They also find the farthest distance equals the diagonal AC. The section then develops a key construction technique: using the compass to transfer a length without a ruler — opening the compass to span a segment and swinging an arc to reproduce that length elsewhere. This is applied to construct a rectangle that can be divided into two identical squares (where length = twice the breadth), then extended to three identical squares. A series of Construct challenges — Square within a Rectangle, Falling Squares, Shadings, Square with a Hole, Square with More Holes, and Square with Curves — requires students to plan constructions using rough diagrams, determine side lengths from symmetry and divisibility constraints, and use the compass-length-transfer technique to achieve precision without trial and error.\n\nSection 8.5 reasons about the properties of diagonals of rectangles and squares through measurement and structured exploration. The section introduces the diagonals PR and QS of rectangle PQRS and asks students to first predict and then measure their lengths, discovering that the diagonals of a rectangle are equal. It then examines how each diagonal divides each pair of opposite angles into two smaller angles, labelling them a–h, and asks students to identify which pairs are equal. An Explore prompt asks how a rectangle must be constructed so that its diagonal divides opposite angles into equal halves, leading students to the answer that this occurs when the rectangle is a square. A structured table records sides and the eight angles formed by the two diagonals across different rectangles. A Math Talk prompt asks students to frame general laws about diagonal angles and sides and discuss how one can be sure they always hold.\n\nSection 8.6 applies the intersection of two circular arcs — each centred at one of two given points — to locate a third point equidistant from both, and uses this technique to construct a House figure with all sides 5 cm. The section opens with the House construction problem, where the apex A must be exactly 5 cm from both B and C, the two top corners of the rectangular walls. It shows that drawing a full circle of radius 5 cm centred at B yields all points 5 cm from B, and a second circle of radius 5 cm centred at C yields all points 5 cm from C; the intersection of the two circles is the required point A. The efficient Method 2 uses only the relevant arcs rather than full circles. Having located A, the arc from A touching B and C completes the roof of the house. Construct exercises ask students to build a larger house with 7 cm sides, recreate Artwork figures using the equidistant-point technique, and investigate whether a quadrilateral with all sides equal but not a square can be constructed (a rhombus), using the same arc-intersection method.",
