  "prose_summary": "Section 6.1 reasons about what a sequence of numbers can convey — a form of encoded information — without the actual quantities being given. The motivating context is children standing in a line, each calling out the count of taller children standing in front of them. Two arrangements of the same children produce different number sequences, and students must decode the rule and then reconstruct a valid height arrangement from a given sequence. The section raises and explores structural constraints: for instance, whether a person can legitimately say '0' only if they are the tallest, or whether the first person always says '0', distinguishing 'always true', 'only sometimes true', and 'never true' statements.\n\nSection 6.2 defines parity (odd/even) and reasons about the parity of sums using the pictorial 'pair arrangement' representation. A sum of any even numbers is always even because each even number can be arranged in pairs; two odd numbers added together are always even because the two 'leftover singles' pair up; three odd numbers always sum to an odd number. These visual proofs lead to the resolution of Kishor's puzzle: five odd numbers cannot sum to 30 (an even number), so no solution exists. Consecutive-number pairs always consist of one even and one odd, so their sum is always odd — used to refute Maria's claim that sibling ages sum to 112. The section then applies parity reasoning to algebraic expressions (e.g. 3n + 4 can be odd or even depending on n, while 100p is always even) and derives that 2n gives the nth even number and 2n – 1 gives the nth odd number.\n\nSection 6.3 reasons through grid-filling constraints and magic square construction, then applies algebraic generalisation. Students fill 3 × 3 grids with non-repeating digits 1–9 to match given row and column sums, and discover the impossibility of a grid with a sum outside [6, 24]. The observation that all row sums add to 45 (= 1 + 2 + ... + 9) and all column sums also add to 45 is established. Magic squares (equal row, column, and diagonal sums) are defined, and students are guided through three observations: the magic sum must be 15, the centre must be 5, and 1 and 9 must be in edge (not corner) positions. A generalised algebraic form of the magic square is constructed using m for the centre and expressing all other entries as m ± k, and used to build squares for any centre value. Historical context covers the Chautīsā Yantra (10th-century Khajuraho inscription), the Lo Shu Square, and the Navagraha and Kubera Yantras.\n\nSection 6.4 reasons through the discovery and structure of the Virahāṅka–Fibonacci sequence by returning to its historical origin in Sanskrit and Prakrit poetic rhythm. The key question is: in how many ways can 8 beats be filled with short (1-beat) and long (2-beat) syllables? The section builds the count systematically for n = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 by the recursive argument that any n-beat rhythm begins with either a 1 or a 2, linking the n-beat count to the (n–1)-beat and (n–2)-beat counts. This gives the sequence 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21, 34, 55, …. The historical priority of Virahāṅka (c. 700 CE) over Fibonacci (1202 CE) is established, and the parity pattern of the sequence (O, E, O, O, E, O, O, E, …) is explored. The section links these numbers to petal counts of daisies as a natural occurrence.\n\nSection 6.5 applies digit-substitution reasoning to cryptarithms — arithmetic problems where each letter stands for a unique digit (0–9). Two worked cases are shown: T + T + T = UT (where T = 5, U = 1) and K2 + K2 = HMM (deducing M and then H). Students then solve four further cryptarithms — YY + Z = ZOO; B5 + 3D = ED5; KP + C1 = KP + C = PRR; 1FF — using logical elimination. The final Figure it Out collects eleven problems drawing on all sections of the chapter: light-bulb toggle parity, encyclopaedia page-sum feasibility, 2 × 3 parity-constrained grid fill, zero-sum magic square with negatives, parity of odd/even counts, parity of 1–100 sum, Virahāṅka sequence neighbours, staircase path count, parity of the 20th Virahāṅka term, true/false expression statements, and a cryptarithm solution.",
