  "prose_summary": "Section 8.1 recalls and applies the multiplication of fractions. The section opens by revisiting whole-number multiplication as repeated addition, then extends it to fraction × whole-number and whole-number × fraction via the number-line distance model (Aaron's walking pace). The two-step procedure for fraction × whole-number — divide the multiplicand by the denominator, then multiply by the numerator — is developed explicitly. Two named examples are worked: Example 1 (farmer distributes 2/3 acre to 5 grandchildren: 5 × 2/3 = 10/3) and Example 2 (internet cost for 5/4 hours at ₹8/hour: 10). The section then extends to fraction × fraction using the unit-square area model: dividing 2/5 into 4 equal parts yields 2/20, then multiplying by 3 gives 6/20. The rectangle-area interpretation (area = product of fractional sides) is made explicit and linked to the general formula a/b × c/d = (a×c)/(b×d), attributed to Brahmagupta's Brāhmasphuṭasiddhānta (628 CE). Cancellation of common factors before multiplying (apavartana) is introduced with a historical note crediting Jaina scholar Umasvati (c. 150 CE). A Math Talk uses the unit-square model to multiply 5/4 × 3/2 = 15/8 (mixed-fraction multiplicand). The section closes by exploring when the product is greater than, between, or less than the two factors — depending on whether each factor is between 0 and 1 or greater than 1 — and confirms commutativity via the rectangle-area argument.\n\nSection 8.2 reasons through division of fractions using the reciprocal and then applies Brahmagupta's division formula. The section opens by restating division as a multiplication question: a ÷ b = ? means b × ? = a. This is applied step by step to 1 ÷ 2/3, 3 ÷ 2/3, 1/5 ÷ 1/2, and 2/3 ÷ 3/5 — in each case identifying the number that cancels the divisor (the reciprocal). The general rule is stated: to divide by a fraction, multiply by its reciprocal; the divisor's reciprocal is the fraction with numerator and denominator swapped. Brahmagupta's formula a/b ÷ c/d = (a×d)/(b×c) is stated and attributed to 628 CE, with Bhāskara II's 1150 CE clarification also cited. The section then investigates the relationship between dividend, divisor, and quotient: when the divisor is between 0 and 1 the quotient exceeds the dividend; when the divisor exceeds 1 the quotient is less — mirroring the multiplication analysis from section 8.1.\n\nSection 8.3 applies fraction multiplication and division to a sequence of contextual and historical problems. Example 3 (Leena's tea: 1/4 ÷ 5 = 1/20 litre per cup) and Example 4 (Baudhāyana's Śhulbasūtra brick problem: 15/2 ÷ 1/25 = 375/2 bricks) apply division directly. Example 5 (Pṛithūdakasvāmī's four fountains, c. 860 CE) requires computing how many times each fountain fills a cistern in a day (1÷1=1, 1÷1/2=2, 1÷1/4=4, 1÷1/5=5) and summing to 12, giving a combined fill time of 1/12 day. A Math Talk derives the shaded fraction of a nested figure (3/32 of the whole square) using successive multiplication: 1/4 × 1/2 × 3/4 = 3/32. The Bhāskarāchārya dramma problem (from Līlāvatī, 1150 CE) multiplies six fractions to show the miser gave exactly 1 cowrie shell. A 'Pinch of History' traces the theory of fractions from the Śhulbasūtra (800 BCE) through Brahmagupta (628 CE), Śhrīdharāchārya (c. 750 CE), Mahāvīrāchārya (c. 850 CE), Pṛithūdakasvāmī (c. 860 CE), Bhāskara I (629 CE), Bhāskara II (c. 1150 CE), and its transmission through Arab mathematicians to Europe. The section's exercises include a rich telescoping-product puzzle (the ant-splitting problem) that generalises to (1−1/2)(1−1/3)…(1−1/n) = 1/n.",
