noting it was encountered in Grade 6. Each side of an equilateral triangle is divided into thirds, an equilateral triangle raised over the middle third, and the middle portion removed — effectively replacing each side with a bump. Students compute the number of sides and the perimeter at each step, practising geometric series reasoning as the perimeter grows without bound while the shape remains bounded.\n\nSection 4.1.4 recalls the presence of fractal-like patterns in human art and architecture across cultures — including the Kandariya Mahadev Temple in Khajuraho (c. 1025 CE), Nigerian Fulani wedding blankets, and the work of M.C. Escher — providing cultural and aesthetic grounding without any computation.\n\nSection 4.2 opens the second major theme by introducing the idea that seeing a solid means seeing its profile from a specific viewpoint, and that the same solid can produce very different profiles depending on the direction of viewing. This framing motivates the visualisation exercises that follow and connects everyday experience (shadows, cartoons, photographs) to mathematical structure.\n\nSection 4.2.1 applies spatial visualisation through a set of imagination-only exercises — no paper drawing allowed. Students mentally picture their name reversed, cut corners from imaginary squares and triangles, and identify the resulting shapes. They then apply this skill to solids: identifying a solid whose profile is a square, a circle, or a triangle, and imagining solids with contrasting profiles from two perpendicular viewpoints (rectangular–circular, circular–triangular, rectangular–triangular, trapezium–circular, pentagonal–rectangular).\n\nSection 4.2.2 recalls and applies the vocabulary and properties of common three-dimensional solids — cuboid, parallelepiped, cylinder, cone, prism, pyramid, tetrahedron, octahedron, and dodecahedron — including faces, edges, and vertices. It then introduces the concept of a net (the flat shape obtained by unfolding a solid) and applies it by having students identify valid nets of a cube (including the Try This task for the regular tetrahedron and square pyramid), draw nets of cuboids with specified dimensions, reason about nets of a cylinder and cone, and make physical cutouts to verify. The section also notes that a cube has exactly 11 distinct nets and an octahedron likewise, and that a dodecahedron has 43,380 nets.\n\nSection 4.2.3 reasons about the shortest path between two points on the surface of a cuboid using the key insight that a path on the cuboid surface corresponds to a path of the same length on any net, so the shortest surface path corresponds to the straight-line path on the appropriate unfolding. The section guides students through two introductory cases (ant–laddu, where one standard unfolding gives a straight line) and then a trickier case (6 × 30 × 12 cm cuboid) where multiple unfoldings must be compared and the Baudhayana–Pythagoras theorem used to compute distances, requiring students to enumerate all distinct unfoldings and select the minimum.\n\nSection 4.2.4 recalls the definition of projection (the perpendicular mapping of a point or object onto a plane) and applies it to reason about projections of lines, squares, parallelograms, polygons, cubes, and cones. The section establishes that projection preserves parallelism — parallel lines project to parallel lines — and introduces the three standard engineering views: front view (vertical plane), top view (horizontal plane), and side view (side plane). It shows that a given projection does not uniquely determine the object (multiple objects can share the same projection), motivating the use of three simultaneous projections.\n\nSection 4.2.5 reasons about the physical counterpart of projection through a shadows experiment: shining a torch perpendicularly at an object near a wall produces a shadow that, as the torch moves infinitely far away, converges to the mathematical projection. The section uses sunlight as the real-world example of a parallel light source producing true projections, and derives that since the projection of a pair of parallel lines is always parallel, the shadow of a parallelogram is always a parallelogram regardless of orientation.\n\nSection 4.2.6 recalls and applies the concept of isometric projection: an orientation of a cube in which the projections of all edges are equal in length. The section explains that balancing a cube on a corner vertex and projecting downward produces this isometric view (a regular hexagon with all edges and their projections equal), and that this structure tiles the plane with hexagons to form the isometric grid used in engineering drawing. Students reason about why the three families of parallel edges map to three directions on the grid.\n\nSection 4.2.7 applies isometric grid drawing to represent three-dimensional solids on paper. The section connects the three axes (height, depth, length) to the three edge directions on the isometric grid (vertical, two diagonals), uses the five Tetris shapes (arrangements of four cubes) as drawing exercises, and shows step-by-step how to draw a 1×1×1 cube and a row of four cubes along various orientations. Students draw given solids on the isometric grid, recreate an impossible path and an impossible triangle (Penrose triangle), and discuss why spatial illusions arise from isometric drawings.",
