  "prose_summary": "Section 7.1 recalls and then reasons about the area of rectangles and squares. The section opens by establishing that area is measured by counting non-overlapping unit squares, and recalls the formula: area of a rectangle = length × width. It then extends this to triangles formed by the diagonal of a rectangle — each triangle is exactly half the rectangle, so its area is (1/2) × length × width. The section then poses the deeper question of why perimeter cannot serve as a proxy for area, reasoning through examples of rectangles with equal perimeters but different areas and vice versa, including a challenge to find two regions where the larger perimeter corresponds to the smaller area. The opening Math Talk prompt asks students to divide a square into four parts of equal area in as many ways as possible, motivating the idea that equal area does not require congruent shape. The dominant acts are first recalling the rectangle area formula, then reasoning about why perimeter and area are independent measures.\n\nSection 7.2 derives and applies the triangle area formula. The section begins by constructing the enclosing rectangle around a triangle with base BC and apex X, showing through congruence arguments that the triangle always occupies exactly half the rectangle, whether the apex lies directly above the base or not. For an obtuse triangle with altitude falling outside BC, the area is derived as the difference of two right-triangle areas, confirming the formula holds universally: area of a triangle = (1/2) × base × height. The section then applies the formula in reverse — given the area and one base, find the altitude to a different base — illustrated with a concrete example of finding altitude BY in triangle ABC when base BC and altitude AX are known (giving BY = 3.75 units). A second application establishes that the diagonals of a rectangle divide it into four triangles of equal area by showing adjacent triangles share the same base (OB = OD) and altitude. The section then extends reasoning to triangles between parallel lines: all triangles with a common base BC and apex on a fixed line l parallel to BC have the same area (same height). Using this, and a mirror-reflection argument, the section locates the triangle of minimum perimeter — the one whose apex lies on the perpendicular bisector of BC — and justifies it rigorously via the law of reflection. The dominant acts are reasoning (proving the universal triangle area formula and the minimum-perimeter result) then applying (computing altitudes from given areas).\n\nSection 7.3 applies the triangle area formula to find the area of any polygon. The section explains that any polygon can be divided into triangles by drawing diagonals, and that by computing the area of each triangle and summing, one can compute the area of any polygon. It illustrates with a quadrilateral divided by diagonal AC and a pentagon subdivided into triangles. The dominant act is applying the triangle decomposition strategy to compute areas of composite polygons.\n\nSection 7.4 derives the area formula for a parallelogram through dissection. The section shows how to convert a parallelogram ABCD into a rectangle of equal area by cutting triangle AXD and placing it at the far end, then justifies the congruence of the cut triangle with the replacement triangle using RHS (right angle, hypotenuse BC = AD, side AX = BY). The term dissection is formally introduced: cutting a figure into pieces and rearranging them to form a different figure of the same area. The formula area of a parallelogram = base × height is derived, and the section confirms that any side may serve as the base (with its corresponding height), by repeating the dissection argument with the other pair of parallel sides. The dominant act is reasoning: constructing the dissection proof and deriving the formula.\n\nSection 7.5 derives the area formula for a rhombus using its diagonal properties. Since a rhombus is a parallelogram, the base × height formula applies, but the section develops a second derivation via dissection of the two isosceles triangles formed by one diagonal. Each triangle is converted into a rectangle of half its area; the two half-rectangles are joined to form rectangle WXYZ. The side lengths of WXYZ are shown to equal the full diagonal AC and half the other diagonal BD. This gives: area of rhombus = (1/2) × product of diagonals. An alternative derivation via the two triangles ADB and CDB (with the perpendicular diagonals providing the altitudes) confirms the same formula. The dominant act is reasoning: constructing the dissection proof and deriving the diagonal product formula.\n\nSection 7.6 derives the area formula for a trapezium through three approaches. The primary derivation breaks trapezium WXYZ (with WX ∥ ZY) into a rectangle WXNM and two right triangles at each end, assigning algebraic labels x, h, a, y to the relevant lengths, and sums the three areas to give (1/2) × h × (x + y + 2a). Substituting b = x + y + a simplifies this to (1/2) × h × (a + b): area of a trapezium = (1/2) × height × sum of parallel sides. The section verifies the formula also holds for a trapezium where one parallel side is longer (two further proof approaches are sketched — rectangle-and-triangle decomposition and a parallelogram-and-triangle split). A third elegant derivation using two congruent copies of the trapezium is then presented: rotating the second copy and joining along a common side produces a parallelogram of base a + b and height h, so the trapezium is exactly half that parallelogram, confirming (1/2)h(a + b). The dominant act is reasoning: deriving the formula through multiple dissection and algebraic paths.\n\nSection 7.7 applies area concepts to real-life measurement contexts. The section works through unit conversions: 1 in = 2.54 cm, so 1 in² = 6.4516 cm²; 1 ft = 12 in; 1 acre = 43,560 ft²; and 1 km² = 1,000,000 m². Students are invited to estimate the area of an A4 sheet, their classroom, school, and city, comparing estimates with actual values. The section also notes that different regions of India use local area units (bigha, gaj, katha, dhur, cent, ankanam). The dominant act is applying area and unit-conversion skills to practical measurement contexts.",
